import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Host Bundles","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Host Bundles","type":"adr","date":"2026-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"Proposed"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0001-host-bundles.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0001-host-bundles.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0001-host-bundles.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="host-bundles" tabindex="-1">Host Bundles <a class="header-anchor" href="#host-bundles" aria-label="Permalink to “Host Bundles”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The handl VM is generic over a <code>Host</code> trait — the kernel defines no runtime, no standard library, no I/O. All functions, control flow, and capabilities come from the host. A host is a <strong>bundle</strong>: a composition of modules that determines what programs can do.</p><p>The same program runs under different hosts with different capabilities. The program declares its needs implicitly through the functions it calls; the host decides whether to fulfill them.</p><p>This ADR catalogs the host bundles we envision, their capability surfaces, and their relationships.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Hosts are categorized by <strong>what they provide</strong> (module set) and <strong>where they run</strong> (execution target). All implement the same <code>Host</code> trait — the VM and language kernel do not change.</p><h3 id="existing-hosts" tabindex="-1">Existing hosts <a class="header-anchor" href="#existing-hosts" aria-label="Permalink to “Existing hosts”">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong><code>DefaultHost</code></strong> — built-ins only: <code>add</code>, <code>mul</code>, <code>concat</code>, <code>len</code>, <code>var</code>, <code>resolve</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>array</code>. Interface-agnostic base layer.</li><li><strong><code>SandboxHost</code></strong> — pure library modules + resource limits (<code>max_depth</code>, <code>max_calls</code>). No I/O, no <code>import()</code>. For untrusted code. Already implemented in <code>handl-host-sandbox</code>.</li><li><strong><code>TypeHost</code></strong> — type-level evaluation. Types are JSON values; <code>is_compatible</code> is an handl function. Meta-circular.</li><li><strong><code>MetaHost</code></strong> — reflection: <code>typeof</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>eval</code>, <code>parse</code>, AST inspection.</li><li><strong><code>CacheHost</code></strong> — decorator that memoizes any inner host. Cross-machine resumability via <code>CallCache</code>.</li><li><strong><code>ResolverHost</code></strong> — decorator that adds runtime <code>import()</code> backed by a <code>ModuleResolver</code>.</li></ul><h3 id="planned-hosts" tabindex="-1">Planned hosts <a class="header-anchor" href="#planned-hosts" aria-label="Permalink to “Planned hosts”">​</a></h3><h4 id="handl-host-sandbox" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-sandbox</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-sandbox" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-sandbox”">​</a></h4><p>Pure functions only. No I/O, no side effects, no <code>import()</code> at runtime (but opt-out from virtual-module system). Deliberately limited for untrusted code. Resource limits (depth, memory, time budget) enforced by the host. A host that provides only pure functions is automatically sandboxed — this is a policy decision, not a language feature.</p><h4 id="handl-host-native" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-native</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-native" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-native”">​</a></h4><p>Full OS access. Composes <code>lib-platform-std</code> (<code>StdFs</code>, <code>StdEnv</code>, <code>StdPath</code>, <code>StdTime</code>, <code>StdHttpClient</code>, <code>StdCmd</code>, <code>StdTmp</code>) + <code>lib-utils</code> + <code>lib-crypto</code> + <code>lib-datetime</code> + <code>lib-rand</code> + <code>lib-intl</code> + <code>lib-kv</code>. The &quot;batteries-included&quot; host for running on a real machine. Long-term reference host. The <code>lib-platform</code> service traits already define the abstract interfaces — <code>handl-host-native</code> wires them to the stdlib implementations.</p><h4 id="handl-host-server" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-server</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-server" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-server”">​</a></h4><p>Long-running server process. Async I/O, connection pooling, request lifecycle. Checkpointing for long computations via <code>Continuation</code>. Composes <code>ResolverHost</code> + <code>CacheHost</code> + <code>lib-platform-std</code> + custom network modules.</p><h4 id="handl-host-edge" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-edge</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-edge" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-edge”">​</a></h4><p>Edge/CDN runtime (Cloudflare Workers, V8 isolates). Cold-start optimized, limited memory, no persistent filesystem. Request/response oriented. Fast instantiation from serialized state.</p><h4 id="handl-host-wasm" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-wasm</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-wasm" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-wasm”">​</a></h4><p>Runs in WebAssembly. Bridges to host environment via imported functions. No direct filesystem or process access — capabilities are delegated to the WASM runtime&#39;s host functions.</p><h4 id="handl-host-js" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-js</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-js" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-js”">​</a></h4><p>Compiles to JavaScript. Provides <code>fetch</code>, <code>localStorage</code>, DOM queries, <code>console</code>, etc. No filesystem, no process spawning. The <code>Continuation</code> mechanism (serializable frame stack) enables SSR patterns: server yields on a <code>fetch</code> operation, ships the continuation to the browser, client resumes with a browser-side <code>fetch</code>. Uses <code>AsyncFunction</code> for network I/O.</p><h4 id="handl-host-web-worker" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-web-worker</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-web-worker" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-web-worker”">​</a></h4><p>Runs in a Web Worker. Offloads computation from the main thread. Post-message-based communication with the main thread. No DOM access. Can use <code>import()</code> via <code>ResolverHost</code> with an in-memory or fetch-based resolver. Suitable for parallel evaluation across workers.</p><h4 id="handl-host-cuda" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-cuda</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-cuda" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-cuda”">​</a></h4><p>Parallel execution. Functions map to GPU kernels/shaders. Batch evaluation across arrays. Different execution model — the VM dispatches to GPU operations instead of sequential function calls.</p><h4 id="handl-host-game" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-game</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-game" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-game”">​</a></h4><p>Game engine embedding. Functions for entity queries, physics, event dispatch, scene graph manipulation. Tight integration with a game loop. The host provides domain-specific functions; the language becomes a scripting layer.</p><h4 id="handl-host-embedded" tabindex="-1"><code>handl-host-embedded</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-host-embedded" aria-label="Permalink to “handl-host-embedded”">​</a></h4><p>Minimal, constrained. For microcontrollers / embedded targets. Stripped module set, no heap allocation beyond what&#39;s needed, no <code>serde_json</code> (custom compact value representation). Maybe even no async. The ultimate &quot;small footprint&quot; host.</p><h3 id="composition-patterns-orthogonal-decorators" tabindex="-1">Composition patterns (orthogonal decorators) <a class="header-anchor" href="#composition-patterns-orthogonal-decorators" aria-label="Permalink to “Composition patterns (orthogonal decorators)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Decorator</th><th>Adds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CacheHost</code></td><td>Memoization layer</td></tr><tr><td><code>ResolverHost</code></td><td>Runtime <code>import()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ModuleHost</code></td><td>Module composition</td></tr><tr><td>Resource-limit wrapper</td><td>Depth / memory / time budgets</td></tr><tr><td>Audit wrapper</td><td>Call logging / observability</td></tr><tr><td>Permission wrapper</td><td>Capability-based access control</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="distinguishing-dimensions" tabindex="-1">Distinguishing dimensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#distinguishing-dimensions" aria-label="Permalink to “Distinguishing dimensions”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I/O access</td><td>none → filesystem → network → process → GPU</td></tr><tr><td>Execution model</td><td>sync → async → lazy → resumable → parallel</td></tr><tr><td>Sandboxing</td><td>full OS → limited → pure only</td></tr><tr><td>Persistence</td><td>stateless → cached → content-addressed → checkpointable</td></tr><tr><td>Target</td><td>native → browser/WASM → embedded → GPU → game engine → edge</td></tr><tr><td>Module set</td><td>empty (<code>DefaultHost</code>) → utils → platform → everything</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>No host is special.</strong> Every host is a bundle of modules implementing the same <code>Host</code> trait. The kernel never needs to know which host it&#39;s running under.</li><li><strong>Capability is emergent.</strong> A program that calls <code>fs.read</code> only works on hosts that provide a filesystem module. The program declares needs implicitly; the host fulfills or rejects them.</li><li><strong>Sandboxing is a policy, not a feature.</strong> A host with only pure functions is automatically sandboxed. No language-level sandboxing mechanism is needed.</li><li><strong>Cross-target portability is real but constrained.</strong> Programs using only pure functions port everywhere. Programs using platform-specific modules port only to hosts that provide those modules.</li><li><strong>New hosts are cheap to build.</strong> Implement <code>Host</code>, compose modules, done. No kernel changes, no language changes, no fork required.</li><li><strong>Host proliferation is a risk.</strong> Too many hosts with overlapping capability surfaces could fragment the ecosystem. Conventions for standard module sets per target should emerge.</li></ul>`,36)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};